sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The lines of growth on bivalve shells are not concentric but coaxial curves through the umbo, and they arise because the rate of growth varies as a simple function of direction; in the simplest case the lines are coaxial circles (as in Anomia), and with more complex functions they are coaxial ellipses or other figures (as in Lima or Solen).";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Bivalve growth lines are coaxial curves set by direction-dependent growth rate";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "r(\\theta, t) = t\\,f(\\theta) from the umbo; f(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta gives coaxial circles";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Lines of growth in bivalves”. Scope: lamellibranch and brachiopod shells with visible growth lines"
}
